    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to the licensing\nboard in the county of Westchester",
    "basePrintNo": "S4222",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4222-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd R3015, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4222\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in\nrelation to the licensing board in the county of Westchester\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo include all relevant professionals licensed by Westchester County in\nthe pleadings requirements of CPLR rule 3015.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends subdivision (e) of rule 3015 of the civil\npractice law and rules to include professionals licensed by Westchester\nCounty under the pleading requirements relating to licenses to do busi-\nness. Section two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to rule 3015 of the Civil Practice Law and Rules (CPLR), where\na plaintiff brings a cause of action against a consumer arising from the\nplaintiff's conduct of a business, which is required by state or local\nlaw to be licensed, such plaintiff's complaint shall allege that he or\nshe was licensed at the time the services in question were rendered. The\ncommon scenario here is a home improvement contractor as plaintiff who\nbrings a claim against a customer to resolve a dispute. Where a consumer\nis sued and the contractor fails to stipulate in their complaint that he\nor she was licensed at the time services were rendered, CPLR rule 3015\nallows the consumer to file a motion to dismiss and the case is\ndismissed.\n\nIn Westchester County, home improvement contractors must be licensed by\nthe county's Consumer Affairs/Weights & Measures Department.  However,\nthe licensing structure for plumbers and electricians in the county is\nunique in that licenses are granted by two separate boards, the West-\nchester County Board of Plumbing Examiners and the Westchester Elec-\ntrical Licensing Board. These are independent boards which are appointed\nby the County Executive.\n\nCurrently, CPLR rule 3015 only applies to those professionals licensed\nby. the Westchester Department of Consumer Affairs/Weights & Measures.\nTherefore, under the county's current licensing structure, the pleading\nrequirements of CPLR rule 3015 apply to home improvement contractors, as\nthey are licensed by the Department of Consumer Affairs/Weights & Meas-\nures, but plumbers and electricians are excluded from this rule.\n\nThis legislation amends CPLR rule 3015 to include all professionals that\nare licensed by Westchester County, not solely those licensed by the\ncounty's Department of Consumer Affairs/Weights & Measures.  Doing so\nwill protect consumers who are sued by a plumber or electrician who\nfails to meet the pleading requirements set out in this section by\naffording them the ability to file a motion to dismiss the case. This\nbill will also align Westchester with the pleadings requirements set out\nunder the current CPLR rule 3015 for counties with similar licensing\nstructures, including Rockland and Putnam counties.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
